WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.010
- Hey, my name is Leslie Odom Jr.

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:02.890
- Eli Goree.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:03.723
- Kingsley Ben-Adir

00:00:03.723 --> 00:00:05.030
- This is Aldis Hodge

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.880
and this is how it became Jim Brown.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:07.900
- I became Malcolm X.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.050
- Became Cassius clay.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.725
- And this is how I became Sam Cooke.

00:00:11.725 --> 00:00:14.308
(upbeat music)

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.090
I remember when I was a little
boy, my dad pointed out to me

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.590
one time the Swan, little
family of swans was grazing

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:31.233
or was slowly like going
like that across on water,

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:34.830
top of the water.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.270
And he said, do you see those?

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.103
Yeah, sure.

00:00:38.103 --> 00:00:39.357
He said, so you see
how graceful they look.

00:00:39.357 --> 00:00:41.283
And he said, underneath the water,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.480
you know, talking about
the amount of effort

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.480
underneath grace, the
amount of effort underneath

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.520
what Sam had crafted,
which was this unflappable

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.520
smooth, professional,
sexy, appealing thing.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.470
there was great effort
holding that all together

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.600
and racking up those accomplishments.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.740
That is the strength of a vision.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.710
So I say that to say the thing
that I learned most about him

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.960
in my discovery, it's
right there in chem script.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.650
And, as I went to do the
research, I discovered,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:26.960
Oh that's why you put it in there.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.980
Sam wasn't nothing to play with.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.250
Sam was a firebrand,
like nobody was pushing

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.030
Sam around anywhere ever.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.560
So yeah, I wanted that
side of him showing,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.980
not just that calm exterior
that we know him for.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.373
- Well, this is off to a hopping start.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.330
- Whenever it's stepping into the shoes

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.950
of someone who was real,
you wanna do your best

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.260
to honor this person
and respect their legacy

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.960
and really celebrate what they've done.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.850
And for me, at first I didn't know

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.490
if I was up to the charge.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.672
I didn't know if I could be here.

00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:01.505
And I was looking out study.

00:02:01.505 --> 00:02:03.050
I was like, can I beat this guy?

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.320
I don't know, So, you know, I didn't know,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.870
but after the audition came
around, the opportunity

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.010
to audition came around a
couple of times I was like,

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:12.930
you know what, let me step
into this, give it a shot

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.460
because there's a reason
it's coming back around.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.300
So, I auditioned and during that process,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.300
I really just looked at at
who he was studied as much

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.070
information as I could when it
came to videos and interviews

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:31.480
and studying his cadence and
how he spoke and how he moved

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.010
and even how he handled altercation.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.430
But mostly it was about
understanding his mentality

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.253
to really get into the mindset
and the essence of Mr. Brown.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.628
- Nice ain't it Malcolm?

00:02:43.628 --> 00:02:45.730
- Its most definitely soothing Cass.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.908
- I Started kind of
working on the character

00:02:50.908 --> 00:02:54.900
a while before this film even
but I had another opportunity

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.100
to do an audition for another
film that I didn't get.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.110
So I just, I was working
on the character for awhile

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.130
but I was looking for a dialect
coach and, and a producer

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.890
from a film I had worked
on before called race.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.510
I was asking her if she knew
any good dialect coaches.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.100
And she introduced me to someone
who introduced me to Trey

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.940
and we worked together from
the first time I'd heard

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.110
about the audition till I
got to meet Regina and then

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.880
all the way through the
shooting process so,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.780
it was pretty long.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.510
It was like, you know, nine
months that we were just

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.900
like working three times a
week for an hour and a half.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.700
And then when we were on set,
probably even more than that

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.010
like every day for about
an hour and we really

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.420
nailed down the Southern
accent and he kind of helped me

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.970
to understand the culture of
the South and how that affects

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.524
the voice, and how that
affects the perspective

00:03:43.524 --> 00:03:45.263
of a young black man.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.990
I think that helped me really
get into the scanner and body

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.890
where, where Cassius was
coming from, from the roots.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.860
And then when we put
everything else on top of that

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.803
foundation, it just felt very
grounded and very natural.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.550
So I think that's probably
when, when I really felt

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.830
like I had a handle on
just the Southern accent.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.160
There was a phrase that
I, it was like a phrase

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.044
that he says all the time to champ.

00:04:11.044 --> 00:04:14.220
- We're all coming back here
for the champs of victory party

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.053
Don't be late.

00:04:15.053 --> 00:04:17.530
- It never felt right
coming out of my mouth.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.993
Like I'm the champ, you
know, I'm the world's champ.

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:21.887
And it was just like, for
some reason it was just,

00:04:21.887 --> 00:04:24.349
it was just awkward for a long time.

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:25.182
And there would be times
when I would say it

00:04:25.182 --> 00:04:26.710
and just be like, did
that sound right to you?

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.980
Did that sound real or authentic?

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.090
And then after a while like
it became more comfortable

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:36.140
but yeah, for some reason
champ just like many times

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.090
it just didn't quite feel right.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.580
- I figured I better start
getting my life story documented

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.980
in my own words while I can.

00:04:44.171 --> 00:04:47.480
- I think a really important
moment was in the reading of it

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.190
before I'd even attempted to
ingest any of the language

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.930
or attempt any of the dialect.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.700
That was, it was reading
Kemps words and really trying

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.670
to understand from an acting point of view

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.834
like what the function of
Malcolm was within this piece.

00:05:03.834 --> 00:05:05.710
And then I read it again and I was like,

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.580
the emotional undercurrent,
his heartbeat is,

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.240
it was a vulnerability
about him in the words

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.880
that felt very unique and I was like

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.680
oh it's just an opportunity,
its a kind of play or explore

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.800
you know Malcolm in a
really different way.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.420
And then I started sort of
investigating what the stakes

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.480
actually were for Malcolm at
this particular moment in time.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.100
And he was on a band and
there was a real sense

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.530
that he was being pushed
out of the nation of Islam

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:41.070
and that his days were numbered.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.260
I think he was beginning
to understand that.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.300
And there was a real sense that his life

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.250
was about to be in real danger.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.200
I'm not sure if that the death threats

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.110
were kind of just starting to happen.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:57.570
The FBI were really up his ass in big way.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.020
They were onto him.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:04.020
And I think that was
understanding all of that

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.280
before anything was the
most important thing.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.220
And Regina and I, you know,
when we jumped on the phone

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.360
after she saw my sort of interpretation

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.520
in my audition tapes,
that's what we spoke about.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.550
We spoke about Malcolm and
the father, and the husband

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.500
and the vulnerability of his situation

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.510
at this time was everything.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.680
And then just, you know,
then it was just a pure joy

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.270
because I was kind of set free by lots

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:35.513
of the Gregory's words on Malcolm.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:41.220
X said that Malcolm had
confided in him around this time

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.330
and said that he felt
weak and he felt hollow.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.980
And no one knew the torments
that he went through

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.463
as his relationship with
the nation was ending.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.970
And Dick said that he, you know

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.760
if Malcolm could hear us
now, it'd be embarrassed.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.530
He was really a sweet and bashful man.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.780
And then the laceration
demagogue that we all know

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.210
that kind of stereotypical
image was really a character

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.980
that Malcolm slipped in and out of

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.097
but wasn't the sum total of who he was.

00:07:13.097 --> 00:07:16.600
And when we do see Malcolm on
YouTube and those, you know

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.690
those classic images of
him, so often it's Malcolm

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:24.975
in response to the most
hideous instances of racism,

00:07:24.975 --> 00:07:28.047
just happened that day or the day before.

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:32.520
And we kind of, you know
that image is really

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.100
what so many people know of
him but I was really excited

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:41.100
about the opportunity to
explore the other side.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.253
- We want a world where we
are safe to be ourselves.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.100
- There was one interview,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.730
like I watched so many documentaries,

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:58.290
you know Antoine Fuqua
did a great one called

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.180
"What's My Name."

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:02.070
And there's the Spike Lee
classic of "When We Were Kings."

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:06.350
And I watched lots of
interviews and read books,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.300
blood brothers was the book

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.580
that I went to that gave
me a lot of information.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.322
But probably every day
for at least three months,

00:08:13.322 --> 00:08:17.010
there was an interview
that I found on YouTube

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:20.250
of him when he's about 20
years old before he had

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:24.150
his first professional, his
first championship fight

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.593
on local Louisville radio.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.230
Just an interview with a fighter,

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:33.290
and people were just calling
in asking him questions

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:35.450
for about an hour and a half.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:38.190
And it was really, really
cool because you learn so much

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:42.070
about how he had to balance
this persona with the reality

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.910
of being a black man in the South.

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:48.140
And he was just really, he was so smart.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.750
There was the moment I was
probably listening to it

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.535
for about two weeks every morning

00:08:52.535 --> 00:08:56.260
before I realized that
there's a moment where

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.060
there's a KKK member who
calls in and you don't,

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.990
I didn't realize it at first,
but when you listen back

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:04.160
and listen back then you realize that

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.360
he's threatening him in code.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.530
And Cassius is trying to
maintain his disposition.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.140
This really happened,
You can go look it up.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:15.870
His disposition as this
braggadocious champ,

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.320
but at the same time realizing
his life is on the line

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.450
once he steps outside
of this radio station

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.150
and the way he knew was the conversation

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:24.040
and manages answers to these questions,

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.680
was just, he's a genius, you
know, at such a young age.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.850
So wise beyond his years.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:32.270
So that was an interview
that I listened to every day

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.170
for months and months and
months, not just for the dialect

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.730
but just also to see
how his mind operated,

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:41.270
and how he processed things,
and how we overcame challenges

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:42.830
and how he interacted with fans.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:45.660
There was just so many
gems in that interview.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:49.040
- The goal is for us to really be free.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.100
- I wanted to showcase the
strength and confidence

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.570
of knowing himself and knowing his value.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:10:00.570
When I did research, I realized
how forthcoming he was,

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:04.070
rather forthright he
was about understanding

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:07.290
who he was in this time in a
space where a lot of people

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.123
openly spoke against him because
of the color of his skin.

00:10:11.969 --> 00:10:13.970
They celebrated him on the field,

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.900
but spoke against him behind closed doors

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.230
but he didn't let that deteriorate.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:22.210
He only left ladder to push
him before and understand

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.840
and even share the value of
taking control of his narrative.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.600
His understanding of economic
power in the community.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.790
He allowed it to motivate
him for progress.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.230
So I really wanted to
display that side of this man

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:37.910
who really was quite
intellectual on the subject

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:42.310
of what it means when it
comes to getting control

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:45.930
of your potential and
completely taking charge

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.130
and pushing forward.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.953
- But we are fighting for our lives!

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:53.270
- You know I know what's
going on out there, right?

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.350
- Well, Leslie and I, before
we started shooting that stuff

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:03.100
we kind of agreed between us quietly

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:08.071
that we were both open to
kind of throw in each other

00:11:08.071 --> 00:11:10.430
and trying things out
and playing games and,

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:13.394
you know, just staying
open to the possibility

00:11:13.394 --> 00:11:16.670
of surprising each other.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:18.720
And it was kind of tough at times.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.830
And when we went in and
especially, I went really hard

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:23.910
and heavy on Leslie in that first week

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:25.810
in that we shot that
scene in the room fast

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.030
and I sort of took no prisoners in

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.790
and laid into him relentlessly for a week.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.500
And there was something
really interesting about that.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.460
It was kind of difficult and uncomfortable

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:35.500
cause we didn't know each other.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.897
And it was really the first
few days of filming, but yeah,

00:11:38.897 --> 00:11:43.123
Leslie and I, it was,

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:45.603
yeah, it was a joy to get

00:11:45.603 --> 00:11:49.830
to really try and bring those
scenes to life with him.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.270
- Everything's not so black and white,

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:52.590
like you make it out to be.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.700
- I mean, the argument
was all laid out for us

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:58.520
in Kemp's beautiful script.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:02.680
I recognize that Kemp wanted
to do something daring.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:06.750
He wanted to have a private
conversation publicly.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.870
These are conversations that
I witnessed first as a boy,

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.870
I witnessed my parents
and adults around me

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:13.510
having conversations like this.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.860
And as an adult, I started
to have them myself.

00:12:19.857 --> 00:12:22.250
And there was something a little dangerous

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:23.083
about this script.

00:12:23.083 --> 00:12:25.210
I couldn't quite put my finger on it,

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:29.230
cause usually the way that we
push boundaries in film are,

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:32.840
I mean, the usual ways I think
are probably like violence

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:36.630
or sex, you know, it's very rare.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:41.630
It is a boundary pushed in
film through an argument,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.880
through talking dialogue.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.570
So, I thought that was really exciting.

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:52.570
And yeah, it's just, I guess the age old

00:12:53.099 --> 00:12:56.318
back and forth of can you, is
the only way to change things.

00:12:56.318 --> 00:12:57.700
There are people that believe you have

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.880
you can only change
things from the inside.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:05.880
And, there's the other adage
that says, you have to divest.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:11.850
You can't build anything
worthwhile in your master's house.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.270
You know that you have to go

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:16.070
and build your own thing outside of that.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:21.070
So, it's a real push and
pull the only not fight

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.460
that I had with, with
brother Kemp but I remember

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.930
because Kingsley as
Malcolm having coming at me

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.340
in that hotel room for days
before I got to respond

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.530
might just land into me.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:37.810
And there's really nowhere for that to go,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.170
for that tension to go
until you get to respond

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.390
the tension just lives in your body.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.680
So there was this one
weekend before I shot

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:46.780
my side of the argument
and I was like, Kemp,

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.820
I need more, but you got it.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:52.200
Like, cause because Malcolm
has compelling arguments,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.200
he's brilliant.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:55.510
And Kingsley's is brilliant at playing.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.973
- Your record is gonna
stand the test of time.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:04.140
- For me to widen out my
face, I wore these plumpers,

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:07.170
which I fit between my jaws on both sides

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.170
what made it look a little
interesting to try to eat.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:13.330
But I had those in my
mouth all day to speak

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.180
and try to give me a little roundup.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:18.930
So you kind of have to work
around how you speak with that

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:22.540
because it's like basically
having a weird set of braces.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:25.420
And yeah, they sometimes they
painted on some of the scars

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:26.950
but I actually had some bumpy knuckles.

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:28.524
So, some of that was real.

00:14:28.524 --> 00:14:30.010
(laughing) That was real.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.726
And then I just had to
size up a little bit.

00:14:32.726 --> 00:14:35.330
I had to get in the gym
a little bit more for,

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.462
cause you know, the small
brother, you know he was swollen.

00:14:38.462 --> 00:14:41.430
So, I had to get up and able to push ups.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:44.250
You know, I'm confident
in my skill set but I,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.181
a part of that confidence
comes a little bit

00:14:46.181 --> 00:14:48.660
of assurance comes afterwards.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.250
While I'm working, I'm
always maintaining the fact

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.610
that I need to do the work
and I never get comfortable.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:56.080
So I never had a moment
where I said, you know what?

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:58.015
I'm in it, admit this is it.

00:14:58.015 --> 00:15:03.015
I felt like, keep up the
work, never let your God down,

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:04.730
keep going because you can do better.

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.140
You can do more, you can
keep pushing further.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.930
And that sort of repetition allows me

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:13.010
to stay in the constant
state of doing work

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.583
so I can keep practicing who he is.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:19.313
- Oh my goodness!

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:25.996
Why am I so pretty?

00:15:25.996 --> 00:15:27.590
(everyone groans)

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:30.630
I hired Trey personally, out of my own,

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.020
before I had the role, I
hired him as my dialect coach.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:34.330
I just said, I know what am,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.310
I believe I'm going to play this role.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.750
And he thank God was
like, okay, I'm on board.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:40.590
Let's do it.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.090
And so I was paying him out of pocket

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:45.110
before I had the role for months.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.840
And then when I got the role,
thank God they brought him on

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.610
as the dialect coach for the film.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:53.793
And then he was a blessing
to all of us from then.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:56.700
But, you know, in same
thing with the boxing,

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:58.950
like I was training at
East Side Boxing Center

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.610
when I was in Vancouver, when
I would shoot on Riverdale.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.234
And then when I'd be in LA,

00:16:03.234 --> 00:16:06.293
I would go to different
boxing clubs around LA.

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:09.970
There was a UFC gym out in Torrance

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.320
where I worked with guys.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.630
There was Mario,

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:15.463
I can't remember his name

00:16:15.463 --> 00:16:18.720
but he's Gabriel Rosado's
personal trainer.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:21.560
He helped me with my boxing stuff.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.130
Anybody I knew that knew
anything about boxing,

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:24.963
I was, I would try to do
anything I could to just pick up

00:16:24.963 --> 00:16:29.963
anything I could, because I
knew I wanted to play this role.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:34.600
And then from when I got
the role, I got introduced

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.840
to Rob Sally who did
the fight choreography

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:40.470
for Creed and for the
Claressa Shields story,

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.510
and is a former professional fighter,

00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:46.930
a current professional fight
coach and was just phenomenal

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:50.116
and has been a wealth
of knowledge ever since.

00:16:50.116 --> 00:16:53.553
- ♪ All together yeah♪ . ♪

00:16:53.553 --> 00:16:56.360
- Yeah I know, that was,
it's a weird thing for actors

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.220
because we look at a script and you see

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.550
something like that a moment.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:01.320
And then, you know Sam sings A Change

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:02.470
Is Gonna Come on the Tonight Show.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.040
You're like, I wanna do that.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:08.020
And then right on the
flip side you're like,

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:09.472
but no time soon though,

00:17:09.472 --> 00:17:12.020
(laughing) like make
that as late as possible.

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:16.760
And so since this is about
becoming, I'll say on that day

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.540
that was my last day of
shooting in new Orleans.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:24.137
And Sam was never closer to
me than he was on that day.

00:17:24.137 --> 00:17:28.260
And that is because, the way I became Sam,

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:33.260
the way that I found my Sam
was through the interrogation

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:36.420
of Kingsley Ben-Adir as Malcolm X

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:40.070
through those relationships
that I developed with Jim

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:43.960
and Cassius and those, you know
my brothers on on that set,

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:48.310
they helped tell me who
I was supposed to be.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:53.020
I was Sam in relation to
these three other guys.

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:54.123
- Look at this view.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:59.717
I bet they're doing
them for me (laughing).

00:18:03.164 --> 00:18:06.164
(soft upbeat music)

